ng project of  mine which may be a bit arbitrarily  simple but I know strangely important to  society right now I don't know but  nonetheless the project's can help  convey some ideas that are this  conversion is going to  or happen so one of the great things  about typescript is that it is very  configurable so we can make it as strict  or as loose as we want in terms of type  checking so a common problem I've seen  is people trying to convert projects  over to typescript and basically trying  to do it all in one go with the strict  configuration so like the initial setup  of that is is way more daunting than it  probably should be so that's what we're  splitting up into three particular steps  so each of the three steps that we're  going to talk about today could be the  end of your typescript conversion and  you'll get a certain level of benefits  at each at each step so we're gonna go  from the loosest configuration to the  strictest and then you can make your own  judgments about what makes sense for you  and your team so on to step one we're  calling it just make it work so the goal  for the first step is simply to get our  code running again without having to  make any significant changes to the code  itself so we'll need to set up a basic  config file so that type strip can know  what rules we want to follow or not  follow and as I mentioned before this  will be the loosest configuration of  typescript so here's an example TS  config file for react native this is  this would live in the root of your  project so it has a couple of rules  we're gonna gloss over for the sake of  time but I want to focus on one in  particular setting no implicit any to  false will allow implicit in ease so  these implicit in ease are used in  typescript when it can't infer a  particular type of a variable so we're  allowing these in the first step just to  get our code to our project to compile  with typescript without making any  significant changes to the code itself  so the next thing we need to do is is  just gonna involve changing the file  extensions over from Jas to TS and tsx  so one slight gotcha with typescript is  that anytime you want to use JSX in your  code the file has to end and dot tsx  which is a bit stricter than how  javascript might work so if you have a  larger project that isn't just to a to  screen app with cats manually changing  this over could be a bit more daunting  but luckily there's an excellent tool  and perfectly title tool called  js2 TS converter that handles changing  these file extensions over as well as  parsing the file for any JSX that that  might be in the in the file and naming  that with the dot TSX extension in that  case so once we're done changing the  file extensions over we're working with  a real type script project okay  however there's probably a decent  possibility that you might still be  seeing a few errors which is actually a  good thing so in my example with the cat  app I was really rushed for time you  know had investors calling me they were  like we need this cat app right now and  I was like probably need to write more  tests and they're like no we have to  have the cats and so I had to rush into  the App Store unfortunately I had a few  fundamental JavaScript issues that went  unnoticed before but types trip is now  able to identify those errors  immediately and give me some feedback as  to what the problem is so in this  example you can see I have a log error  function at the top it expects two  arguments however when I'm using it at  the bottom I'm only passing it one  so type strip is able to bring it to my  attention immediately and tell me what  argument I'm missing in the in the call  so once I fix these issues the project  should run successfully and we're  officially done with step one so now for  step two we're gonna be explicit so the  next step in the process is going to be  to only allow explicit innies by  toggling no implicit in e to true so  this means that instead of typescript  defaulting to in any type when it can't  use type inference it's actually gonna  give it a compile error so all the  places that the compiler was  automatically adding it any type will  now require you to give it a type or  explicitly say it's an inning which  contrary to pop contrary to popular  belief using any types is fine use them  when you need to  don't don't feel any pressure to not or  to not use any x'  so you can see here in this example i'm  saying that this function expects an  image ID and as a string and a value as  a boolean so i would then need to go  through the application and do the same  sort of thing by specifying the argument  types for  the different functions and methods of  whatnot so this is also a good time to  start creating the common object types  that are used throughout the project and  adding them more possible so here's a  cat object type you can see all the  properties that it has I'm then going to  use this type all over my project any  time I'm dealing with a cat in the data  so I'm just going to add it as the type  in the function another part of this  step will be to bring in types from  third-party libraries that don't provide  type script types so if the library is  written in regular JavaScript we can  still add types on top of it that  describe its usage so there's an  excellent open-source resource called  definitely typed that contains types for  many popular libraries such as you know  lodash or react or react native itself  even so you just need to add at types  before the library name and then just  add it to your projects appendices so  this step will probably take a bit  longer than that first step so but the  efforts you spend doing this will pay  off dividends once you start adding to  your codebase and especially when you  start refactoring your code so like I  mentioned before any of these steps  could be the end of your types of  conversion so we have many projects at  heirship that ended up somewhere in the  middle of steps one and two and honestly  there are a lot better off because of  that so however if you want to keep  pushing on and go all in on typescript  there's only one more step so we'll call  it strict mode so the last step involves  enabling the strict setting which simply  adds a number of strict rules for  typescript to adhere to even though the  setting might sound a bit invasive and  over-the-top these rules really just  enforced good JavaScript patterns in  your code so one of the rules enabled  with strict is straight null checks so  this was created to ensure that you  don't try to use the variable that could  potentially be null or undefined so for  example we have this cat type from  earlier and you can see that the age  property is optional so you can see that  it's optional because it has a question  mark after the key which means that it  could be  undefined so when I try to run the to  string method on age types was going to  give me or type screen to give me an  error so I need to make sure age exists  first before trying to run that method  otherwise I'm just gonna return a  default string so depending on the  project there might actually not be that  much refactoring for step three at all  if you're already writing safe and safe  JavaScript and there's a high likelihood  that types rip is going to agree as well  so and that's pretty much it so we've  taken a standard react native JavaScript  project and successfully converted over  to type strip and three contain steps so  first we added a basic type script  configuration and simply change the file  extension is over then we got more  explicit about the types we're using  throughout the application by turning  off implicit in ease and creating our  own common types as well as bring in  third-party types and then finally we  enabled this strict configuration which  further ensures that we're writing safe  and air resilient code now I I know the  title of this lightning talk might have  been a bit misleading because a full and  complete conversion to type script with  a robust project that isn't just two  screens about cats you know could take  days weeks or even months to  successfully pour it over but I want to  reiterate the fact that types rip can  add value to your project you know at  any step no matter if you're just  changing the file extensions over and  setting up the config or you're a  hundred percent integrated so I hope  this quick talk has helped clear up some  questions you might have had but as you  might imagine there's a lot more to this  process then then we can fit in in ten  minutes so please come find me  afterwards if you have any more  questions about typescript and react  native so thank you very much for having  me  [Applause]  [Music]
 

